(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["microcirculation"]="<h1>Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-is-the-microcirculation-necessary\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why is the microcirculation necessary?</h2>\n<p>Every cell needs oxygen and nutrients, but the big arteries can't deliver them directly. The handover happens in the <strong><span data-term=\"microcirculation\">microcirculation</span></strong>: the tiny vessels inside each tissue.</p>\n<div class=\"micro-path\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Microcirculatory sequence\"><span>Small arteriole</span><b>→</b><span>Capillary exchange</span><b>→</b><span>Venule</span><b>→</b><span>Venous return</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Capillaries are built for exchange:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thin walls</strong>, so the distance for diffusion is tiny.</li>\n<li><strong>Huge numbers</strong>, giving an enormous surface area. Almost every cell is within a few cell-widths of one.</li>\n<li><strong>Slow flow</strong>, because their total cross-section is so large, which gives time for exchange. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries\">→ Haemodynamics, §4</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Yet capillaries hold only about <strong>5% of the blood</strong> at any moment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-is-a-capillary-wall-made-of\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> What is a capillary wall made of?</h2>\n<p>A capillary wall is just <strong>one layer of endothelial cells</strong> sitting on a thin <strong>basement membrane</strong>, sometimes wrapped by <strong><span data-term=\"pericytes\">pericytes</span></strong>. There is <strong>no muscle layer</strong>: capillaries can't constrict themselves. That job belongs to the arterioles that feed them.</p>\n<p><strong>Three types</strong>, from tightest to leakiest:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Type</th>\n  <th>What the wall looks like</th>\n  <th>Where</th>\n  <th>What gets through</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Continuous</strong></td>\n  <td>Unbroken lining, with narrow gaps between cells</td>\n  <td>Muscle, skin, lungs; brain (tightest of all)</td>\n  <td>Water, gases and small molecules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"fenestrated\">Fenestrated</span></strong></td>\n  <td>Cells with tiny pores (<em>fenestra</em> = window)</td>\n  <td>Gut villi, endocrine glands, kidney glomeruli</td>\n  <td>Water and small solutes, quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Discontinuous (sinusoidal)</strong></td>\n  <td>Large gaps, broken basement membrane</td>\n  <td>Liver, spleen, bone marrow</td>\n  <td>Even proteins and blood cells</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The tissue's job decides the type. The gut and glands move a lot of fluid quickly, so they have fenestrated capillaries. The liver handles proteins, so it has the leakiest kind. The brain must be protected, so it has the tightest.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> A lecture slide gives the size of gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres wide, far smaller than one cell.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-how-do-substances-actually-cross-the-capillary-wall\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> How do substances actually cross the capillary wall?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Route</th>\n  <th>What crosses</th>\n  <th>What drives it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Diffusion</strong></td>\n  <td>O₂, CO₂, glucose, ions</td>\n  <td>Concentration difference: from more to less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Gaps and pores</strong></td>\n  <td>Water and small water-soluble molecules</td>\n  <td>The same gradients, through the openings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Vesicles</strong></td>\n  <td>Some large molecules</td>\n  <td>The cell carries them across in little bubbles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Filtration</strong></td>\n  <td>Bulk water with its dissolved solutes</td>\n  <td>Pressure differences (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">§5</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Diffusion and filtration are different.</strong> In diffusion, individual molecules move down their own concentration difference: O₂ out to the cells, CO₂ back in. In filtration, water as a whole is <strong>pushed</strong> out by pressure. Most gas and nutrient exchange is diffusion.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why are capillaries so good at exchange?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Their thick walls protect the solutes as they cross</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Their fast flow brings fresh blood to cells quickly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Their muscle layer squeezes solutes out into tissue</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> A short diffusion distance, a huge surface area and plenty of time all favour exchange.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which capillaries let even proteins and blood cells through?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The continuous capillaries of the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The fenestrated capillaries of the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The continuous capillaries of muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The sinusoidal capillaries of the liver</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Sinusoids have large gaps and a broken basement membrane. Brain capillaries are the tightest.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> How does oxygen get from capillary blood into a working muscle cell?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. By diffusion, from higher to lower concentration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. By filtration, pushed out by blood pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Carried across inside vesicles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pumped across by the endothelial cells</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The cell uses oxygen, keeping its own level low, so oxygen diffuses out of the blood towards it.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-decides-which-capillaries-receive-more-blood\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> What decides which capillaries receive more blood?</h2>\n<p>Capillaries have no muscle, so they can't control their own flow. Flow is set <strong>upstream</strong>, by the <strong>arterioles</strong> (and, in classic teaching, by rings of muscle at the capillary entrances: <strong><span data-term=\"precapillary-sphincter\">precapillary sphincters</span></strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Active tissue calls for more blood.</strong> Working muscle releases metabolites (CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺) that relax the nearby arterioles. More blood flows in, and <strong>more capillaries open up</strong> (recruitment), so more surface area is available for exchange. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Regulation</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Precapillary sphincters are classic teaching, but they are not found at every capillary. In many tissues the arterioles do the controlling.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood?</h2>\n<p>Fluid movement across the capillary wall is a <strong>tug-of-war between two kinds of pressure</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"hydrostatic-pressure\">Hydrostatic pressure</span></strong> is the <strong>push</strong> of fluid against the wall. Blood pressure inside the capillary (<strong>Pc</strong>) pushes water <strong>out</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"oncotic-pressure\">Oncotic pressure</span></strong> is the <strong>pull</strong> of proteins. Proteins can't cross the wall easily, and they hold water with them. Plasma proteins, mainly <strong>albumin</strong>, pull water <strong>in</strong> (<strong>πc</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The tissue side has its own, smaller, versions of each:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Force</th>\n  <th>Symbol</th>\n  <th>Direction</th>\n  <th>Typical size (classic values)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Capillary blood pressure</td>\n  <td><strong>Pc</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Out</strong></td>\n  <td>About 30 mmHg (arterial end), falling to about 10 (venous end)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Plasma protein pull</td>\n  <td><strong>πc</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>In</strong></td>\n  <td>About 28 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Tissue fluid pressure</td>\n  <td><strong>Pi</strong></td>\n  <td>Usually slightly out (it is slightly below zero)</td>\n  <td>About −3 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Tissue protein pull</td>\n  <td><strong>πi</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Out</strong></td>\n  <td>About 8 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does fluid leave even though proteins stay in?</strong> Because the <strong>push out</strong> (Pc, with help from the tissue forces) beats the <strong>pull in</strong> (πc), at least at the arterial end. The proteins stay behind, but water still filters out.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure micro-force-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Hydrostatic and oncotic influences on capillary filtration</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 570 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original schematic capillary containing plasma proteins, with an outward hydrostatic arrow from blood to tissue and an opposing inward plasma oncotic arrow.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"51\" width=\"490\" height=\"76\" rx=\"30\" fill=\"#f5d8df\" stroke=\"#b66b80\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g fill=\"#ae536c\"><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"327\" cy=\"88\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"458\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/></g><path d=\"M239 77 V14 M230 27 L239 14 L248 27\" stroke=\"#d27c26\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M389 14 V75 M380 62 L389 75 L398 62\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"166\" y=\"13\">Pc: outward</text><text x=\"400\" y=\"13\">Plasma oncotic: inward</text><text x=\"167\" y=\"96\">Capillary blood</text><text x=\"181\" y=\"151\">Interstitial tissue space</text></g></svg><p>A conceptual comparison of two important forces. Actual net filtration also depends on interstitial forces, surface area, permeability and the endothelial glycocalyx.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"starling-balance\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Starling balance</figcaption><p>One capillary drawn left (arterial end) to right (venous end). At each end, an outward arrow shows the push (Pc) and an inward arrow the pull (πc), sized to scale, with the net result: \"fluid leaves\" or \"fluid returns\". Sliders for capillary pressure and plasma protein. Scenario buttons: Normal, Heart failure (Pc ↑), Low albumin (πc ↓), Leaky capillary (proteins escape, so the pull weakens). A line underneath states the net effect in plain words. Classic textbook values.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why does albumin matter so much?</strong> It provides most of the pull. If albumin falls (liver disease, kidney protein loss, malnutrition), the pull weakens, more fluid stays out, and tissues swell. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Classic Starling model versus revised understanding</h2>\n<p><strong>The classic model (the lecture's):</strong> the push wins at the arterial end, so fluid filters out. At the venous end, Pc has fallen below the protein pull, so fluid is drawn back in. Of about <strong>24 L filtered a day, about 85% is reabsorbed</strong> into the capillaries, and <strong>15% is returned by the lymph</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What we now know:</strong> the inner surface of the capillary is coated with a gel-like layer, the <strong><span data-term=\"glycocalyx\">glycocalyx</span></strong>. The protein pull that matters is the one across this layer, and it stays strong along the whole capillary. So in most tissues, <strong>little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end</strong>. Instead, a small net filtration continues all along, and the <strong>lymphatics return almost all of it</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to learn:</strong> know the four forces and the lecture's classic numbers for the exam. But understand that <strong>the lymphatics are the main return route, not a back-up</strong>. That is why blocked lymphatics cause swelling so readily. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/lymphatics\">→ Lymphatics</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The 24 L/day and 85%/15% figures are the lecture's classic model. The revised (glycocalyx) model is current physiology (Levick and Michel, 2010).</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-microvascular-filtration-or-permeability\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> What changes microvascular filtration or permeability?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Change</th>\n  <th>Effect on filtration</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arterioles dilate</strong></td>\n  <td>↑</td>\n  <td>More upstream pressure reaches the capillary, so Pc rises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Veins congested or blocked</strong></td>\n  <td>↑</td>\n  <td>Blood backs up, so Pc rises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Leaky capillaries</strong> (histamine, bradykinin, substance P)</td>\n  <td>↑</td>\n  <td>Proteins escape, weakening the pull and widening the gaps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>More capillaries open</strong></td>\n  <td>↑ total exchange</td>\n  <td>More surface area</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Two very different causes of swelling:</strong> a <strong>raised pressure</strong> (a blocked vein) pushes more water through a normal wall. A <strong>leaky wall</strong> (inflammation) lets water and proteins through even at normal pressure. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema\">→ Oedema</a>)</em></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–7\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–7 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Capillary blood pressure rises with nothing else changed. What happens to filtration?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because the push out is stronger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls, because the pull in is stronger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It stays the same, because proteins stay in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It reverses, so fluid is drawn back in</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Pc is the main outward force. Raising it increases the net push out.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient's plasma albumin is very low. Why might their tissues swell?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Albumin pushes water out of capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Capillary blood pressure rises when albumin falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Less protein in the blood weakens the pull holding water in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The lymphatics stop working without albumin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Albumin provides most of the plasma oncotic pull. With less of it, more fluid stays out in the tissues.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In the revised (glycocalyx) model, how does most filtered fluid get back to the blood?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It is reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is returned through the lymphatics</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It evaporates through the skin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is excreted by the kidneys</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end in most tissues. The lymphatics are the main return route.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Capillaries exchange</strong>: thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow; they hold only about 5% of the blood.</li>\n<li><strong>The wall</strong> is one layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, with no muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Three types:</strong> continuous (tightest in the brain), fenestrated (gut, glands, kidney), sinusoidal (liver, spleen, marrow).</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong> moves gases and nutrients down their gradients. <strong>Filtration</strong> pushes bulk water out with pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterioles and precapillary sphincters</strong> control which capillaries get blood. Active tissue opens more.</li>\n<li><strong>Starling tug-of-war:</strong> Pc pushes out, πc (albumin) pulls in; the tissue forces Pi and πi are smaller.</li>\n<li><strong>Classic model:</strong> filtration at the arterial end, reabsorption at the venous end; 24 L/day, 85% back to the capillaries, 15% to the lymph.</li>\n<li><strong>Revised model:</strong> the glycocalyx keeps the pull strong, so little is reabsorbed, and <strong>the lymph returns most filtered fluid</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Filtration rises</strong> with higher Pc (arteriolar dilation, venous congestion), lower πc (low albumin) or a leaky wall (histamine, bradykinin, substance P).</li>\n</ol>\n";
